{"text": " The same task briefed two different ways to the same AI model produces outputs so different they look like they came from different tools. The only variable is context and role. This lesson shows you exactly how to use both. In this lesson how role calibrates vocabulary expertise and perspective and how context eliminates the assumptions that produce generic useless output. We cover why role is the highest leverage component in any prompt. How to write a role that actually changes the output quality? What context does that task description alone cannot? And the assumption problem why AI always fills gaps with the average. Role and context work together to eliminate the two biggest sources of poor AI output. Role eliminates expertise ambiguity. It tells AI exactly what knowledge base, vocabulary and perspective to draw from. Context eliminates situation ambiguity. It tells AI the specific circumstances, constraints and objectives that make this task different from the generic version. Before writing any prompt ask yourself two questions. First, what specific expert would produce the best response to this task? Make that your role. Second, what does that expert need to know about my specific situation that they could not know by default? Make that your context. Remember this. Role sets the expertise level. Context sets the situation. Together they eliminate the two biggest sources of generic output. Neither works without the other. Apply what you learn in this lesson and you will see results immediately. Your skills bear fruit.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 8.48, "text": " The same task briefed two different ways to the same AI model produces outputs so different", "tokens": [50364, 440, 912, 5633, 5353, 292, 732, 819, 2098, 281, 264, 912, 7318, 2316, 14725, 23930, 370, 819, 50804], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15199110800759835, "compression_ratio": 1.5897435897435896, "no_speech_prob": 0.011538105085492134, "words": [{"